(Sec.~\ref{sec:seed}).
\end{table*}

\textbf{FLAME's robustness is a false-positive story.} It reaches BSR $0.010$ on
the fixed trigger, the second-best number in the column, while flagging
$0.40\pm0.04$ of \emph{honest} clients---and $0.45$--$0.46$ on the other two
triggers, where its DR is only $0.42$--$0.46$. A defense discarding two honest
updates in five is training on roughly $60\%$ of the federation, which is a
sufficient explanation for its $0.499$ clean accuracy against FedAvg's $0.702$---a
deficit of $0.20$.
This is the mechanism behind the ``no free lunch'' observation, and it is
invisible without FPR: on BSR alone FLAME looks like a near-solution.

\textbf{GradCos's detector is not merely blind on low-salience triggers---it is
uninformative.} On statistical and temporal its DR ($0.11$, $0.14$) is
\emph{below} its FPR ($0.12$, $0.12$). The clique test is not failing to find the
colluders so much as flagging clients at close to chance. We think this is the
sharpest available statement of the blind spot, and it is stronger evidence than
a low DR alone would be, because it rules out the reading that the detector is
simply conservative.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.82\columnwidth]{fig_dr_fpr.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{Detection rate against false-positive rate} (CICIoT2023, IID,
$m{=}0.2$, $p{=}0.1$, 5 seeds). Only GradCos on the fixed trigger reaches the
top-left corner a detector should occupy. FLAME sits far right on every
trigger---its low backdoor rate is paid for by discarding honest updates---and
GradCos on the low-salience triggers sits \emph{on} the chance diagonal, flagging
honest and malicious clients at indistinguishable rates. FedAvg and the
coordinate-wise rules never flag a client and do not appear.}
\label{fig:drfpr}
\end{figure}

\textbf{FLTrust never excludes anyone.} Its DR and FPR are identically zero on
every trigger: no client's update ever receives a trust score of exactly zero.
Whatever robustness it has comes from continuous reweighting toward the server's
root direction, not from exclusion---which is also why it degrades the clean
model, since honest updates that disagree with a 300-sample root direction are
attenuated too.

\subsection{Salience decides the outcome, and it is measurable}
\label{sec:salience}

Grouped by trigger rather than by attacker count, the pattern in
Table~\ref{tab:main} is stark. On the \emph{fixed} trigger the direction- and
reference-based defenses work: GradCos $0.004$ and FLTrust $0.004$ are lowest,
FLAME $0.010$, against FedAvg's $0.968$. On \emph{statistical} and
\emph{temporal} they fail almost completely---GradCos, Krum and FoolsGold all at
$1.00$, FLAME at $0.86$---with FLTrust on statistical ($0.007$) the lone
exception, and it uses its clean root direction rather than inter-client
similarity.

The mechanism is visible in the update geometry we log. The fixed trigger stamps
header/flag features that few benign flows exercise, so learning it bends a
poisoned client's update in a direction honest clients never take. The
statistical and temporal triggers perturb volume, dispersion and timing features
and leave the poisoned update nearly collinear with an honest one, so the cosine
signal every one of these defenses depends on is simply not there. That FoolsGold
---built precisely to catch colluding sybils by cosine similarity---also fails
underlines that the blind spot belongs to the \emph{signal}, not to one defense's
tuning.

\textbf{Identifying the axis.} A hostile reading of the paragraph above is that
it is nearly definitional: ``direction-based defenses fail when the trigger does
not move the direction'' restates the mechanism rather than testing it. Two
things separate the claim from a tautology. First, $S$
(Eq.~\ref{eq:salience}) is measured independently of any defense's decision, so
BSR and DR can be regressed on it. Second, the three trigger families differ in
\emph{which} features they touch as well as how hard, so a two-group contrast
cannot separate feature coverage from magnitude. We therefore sweep the trigger
magnitude at a fixed feature set, $0.25\times$ to $3\times$ the base pattern, and
report BSR and DR against measured $S$ rather than against the trigger's name
(Fig.~\ref{fig:dose}). \PENDING

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
% Generated by paper/make_figs.py once lane 2 of run_remediation.sh completes.
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_detection.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{Dose--response along the measured salience axis.} \PENDING{}
Placeholder shows the current detection-vs-BSR figure. The final version plots
BSR and DR against the measured salience gap $S$ for trigger magnitudes
$0.25$--$3\times$ at a fixed feature set, with the clean-model BSR overlaid to
mark where a large trigger stops being a learned backdoor and becomes an
out-of-distribution artifact.}
\label{fig:dose}
\end{figure}

\subsection{The collusion signal is cheap to remove}
\label{sec:adaptive}

Every direction-based rule here detects colluders because colluders resemble one
another: GradCos's clique test flags a client for having a near-twin, FoolsGold
penalizes similar histories, FLAME's clustering separates a coherent minority.
That shared premise is the attack surface, and removing it costs the attacker
almost nothing.

Let $M$ be the malicious cohort and $\delta_j$, $j\in M$, their updates. The
attacker draws independent isotropic noise $\nu_j$ scaled to $\lambda\|\delta_j\|$
and subtracts the cohort mean,
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:evasion}
\tilde\delta_j \;=\; \delta_j + \Big(\nu_j - \tfrac{1}{|M|}\textstyle\sum_{k\in M}\nu_k\Big),
\end{equation}
so the perturbation sums to zero over $M$ and contributes nothing to the
aggregate, while each pair of malicious updates is decorrelated. There is no
optimization and one scalar knob, $\lambda$. We call this variant
\texttt{decorrelated}; it inflates the malicious update norms, so a magnitude
rule could still see it. The \texttt{adaptive} variant additionally rescales each
malicious update to the round's median honest norm, hiding that tell as well.

The evasion does what it is designed to do, and this can be checked without
running a single defense, because the quantity it targets is one we log. Measured
on the malicious block, the mean off-diagonal cosine among colluders falls from
$0.99$ to $0.16$--$0.33$ at $\lambda=1$ while the honest cohort's stays at
$0.99$; the median malicious-to-honest norm ratio stays near $1$ under the
norm-matched variant, against $\approx1.23$ for the noise-only variant, which is
the magnitude tell the norm-matching exists to hide; and the malicious mean
update---the component the backdoor rides on---is preserved. The colluders stop looking like each other and keep
contributing the same thing on average, which is precisely the property every
clique-, cluster- or history-similarity test is built to exclude.

\emph{Provenance.} These geometry figures come from a reduced-scale validation
run (6 clients, 3 rounds, 8k rows) used to verify the transform end to end. They
establish that it has the intended effect on the signal; they are \emph{not}
evidence about backdoor outcomes. Full-scale geometry, and every BSR/DR figure
below, come from the queued runs. \PENDING

Table~\ref{tab:adaptive} reports BSR, DR and FPR for the static and adaptive
attackers at the headline point against every direction-based defense, and
Fig.~\ref{fig:evasion} sweeps $\lambda$. \PENDING

