\subsection{Collusion: the malicious-ratio axis}
\label{sec:collusion}

Within the fixed trigger, sweeping the malicious fraction exposes how the
defenses degrade (Table~\ref{tab:ratio}). Krum is the cautionary case: robust at
$10\%$ but collapsing to $0.69$ at $20\%$ and $0.99$ at $30\%$, because once the
malicious set is large enough its members become each other's nearest neighbours
and Krum selects one of them. GradCos, FLAME and FLTrust hold across the range.

This sweep is \textbf{single-seed}, and by our own variance argument
(Sec.~\ref{sec:seed}) single-seed numbers are least trustworthy exactly at
threshold-crossing points like Krum's collapse. We therefore treat it as
directional evidence for an ordering (Krum degrades with $m$ where the others do
not), not as a measurement of where the collapse happens; a three-seed repeat of
this sweep is in the released run plan. \PENDING

