\subsection{No free lunch: the clean-accuracy cost}
\label{sec:tradeoff}

Reading Table~\ref{tab:main} down the clean-CA column reframes the fixed-trigger
result. FLAME and FLTrust reach a low BSR with the weakest clean models
($0.499$ and $0.556$, macro-F1 $0.334$ and $0.426$) against FedAvg's
$0.702/0.561$. GradCos, Krum and FoolsGold keep FedAvg-level clean accuracy. On
the fixed trigger GradCos ties FLTrust's BSR at $+0.15$ clean accuracy.

Sec.~\ref{sec:fpr} supplies the mechanism for half of this: FLAME's deficit is
attributable to discarding $40\%$ of honest updates, not to its noise scale
alone. That distinction matters for the criticism that our clean-accuracy
critique might be a tuning artifact---a badly chosen DP noise scale can crater
clean accuracy by itself. The FPR evidence shows the loss is happening at the
\emph{clustering} stage, upstream of the noise, and the calibration protocol of
Sec.~\ref{sec:tuning} tests the remaining exposure by sweeping the noise scale on
held-out data. Until that run lands we mark this claim as supported for the
clustering mechanism and provisional as to the noise scale. \PENDING

